---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Docs — CareLoop quickstart';
const description =
  'Run CareLoop locally: start the self-hosted MCP server, build the web surfaces, then open the simulator, companion, judge panel and metrics endpoints.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="cl-prose">
    <h1>Quickstart</h1>
    <p class="u-mute">
      CareLoop is a self-hosted MCP server plus a small set of static web surfaces. There is no
      cloud dependency to run the demo: the extraction pipeline falls back to deterministic rules
      when no model is configured.
    </p>

    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Python 3.11+ on your path.</li>
      <li>Node.js 20+ and npm for the web surfaces.</li>
      <li>Optional: AWS credentials and a Bedrock model id for the two-pass LLM extraction. Without
        them the rules-only path is used automatically.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>1. Start the MCP server</h2>
    <p>
      The server exposes the MCP endpoint, the companion capture API, the OAuth endpoints, the
      simulated assistant page, the judge panel and metrics:
    </p>
    <pre class="cl-pre"><code>python -m careloop.mcp_server</code></pre>
    <p class="cl-note">
      It listens on <span class="cl-kbd">http://127.0.0.1:8080</span> by default (override with
      <span class="cl-kbd">HOST</span> / <span class="cl-kbd">PORT</span>). Set
      <span class="cl-kbd">CARELOOP_AUTH=off</span> for the no-credentials demo, or leave the
      default <span class="cl-kbd">oauth</span> mode to exercise the full linking flow.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Build the web surfaces</h2>
    <p>Install workspace dependencies, then build the simulator, the companion and this site:</p>
    <pre class="cl-pre"><code>npm install
npm run dist</code></pre>
    <p class="cl-note">
      <span class="cl-kbd">npm run dist</span> builds each app and syncs the output into the
      server&rsquo;s <span class="cl-kbd">careloop/web</span> directory so it can serve them.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Run the guided demo</h2>
    <p>
      The Makefile wires the pieces into one reproducible demo (seed the demo household, run the
      checks, and exercise the capture-and-ask loop):
    </p>
    <pre class="cl-pre"><code>make demo</code></pre>

    <h2>URL map</h2>
    <p>Once the server is running, these are the routes to know:</p>
    <div class="cl-panel" style="padding-inline:0;overflow:hidden;margin-top:var(--cl-sp-4);">
      <table class="cl-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">CareLoop URL map</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Path</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/</span></th><td>Simulated Alexa+ surface (the default landing view).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/simulator</span></th><td>The assistant simulator — talk to the MCP tools without a device.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/judge</span></th><td>Judge panel: policy self-tests, ledger health, latency and cost.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/companion/</span></th><td>Full-dose companion page, behind the household login.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/mcp</span></th><td>The MCP endpoint (Streamable HTTP, POST).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/metrics</span></th><td>Per-tool latency percentiles and request/error counts.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="cl-kbd">/api/judge-data</span></th><td>JSON backing the judge panel (protocol, tools, ledger, self-tests).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="cl-note" style="margin-top:var(--cl-sp-5);">
      Next: <a href="/docs/architecture">read the architecture</a> for the data flow and the twelve
      MCP tools, or <a href="/docs/judging">the judging map</a> for where each claim is evidenced in
      the repository.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
